// Reports/tree-rewrite-design.md's replacement for the old TreeState-based
// continuous-position space colonization -- emergent, per-cell growth
// instead: a GrowingTip picks among its open 8-neighbours, weighted by
// local field reads (light, wind) plus a canopy-density self-avoidance
// term (deposit -> diffuse -> decay -> follow, not a private attractor
// point cloud), gated on locally diffusing resource rather than an
// unbounded energy pool. See that report for the full reasoning; this
// file is data, not a second place to re-derive it.
//
// GrowingTip carries both Grow and Photosynthesize -- this pass's Grow
// only ever creates more of its own parent's cell type (no separate Leaf
// spawned), so an actively growing shoot is also its own light-catching
// surface, the same way a real young shoot photosynthesizes before it
// lignifies into bark-covered wood. CellType::Leaf stays defined in
// organism.rs for a future species that wants a real distinct leaf stage;
// this file doesn't need it to be a tree.
//
// Every numeric value below is a first pass, deliberately -- resource now
// lives on a bounded 0..RESOURCE_SCALE (4.0) scale, not the old TreeState's
// unbounded energy pool, so the old constants (GROWTH_COST: 4.0,
// AMBIENT_GROWTH_ENERGY: 1.2, ROOT_WATER_ENERGY: 5.0, ...) don't transfer
// directly -- re-tuned for the new scale, calibrated against what actually
// grows in Reports/tree-rewrite-design.md's own live-verification step
// (step 6 of its retrofit order), not assumed correct on paper.
//
// GrowingTip's `Grow.cost`/`Photosynthesize.rate` (0.4/0.35 originally)
// were re-tuned after `examples/debug_tree_variants.rs` compared 6
// candidate pairs planted side by side in one scene, run for 20,000 ticks
// (~5.5 of field.rs's own day/night cycles, not a single lighting
// snapshot): 0.4/0.35 plateaued at 10 wood cells and never branched: even
// doubling income alone (0.4/0.55) still plateaued at 10, but lowering
// cost alone (0.25/0.35) reached 16 -- cost was the dominant lever, not
// average income, because a fresh cell's very first Grow check always
// reads resource=0 and Grow runs *before* Photosynthesize each tick, so a
// lower cost mainly buys margin against an unlucky low-light tick rather
// than raising the average. 0.2/0.5 won clearly (30 cells, real branching
// -- two Y-forks, not just a longer twig) and is what's below. Still a
// first pass in the sense that matters most: tuned entirely in one fixed
// test room (`examples/debug_tree_v2.rs`'s stone floor near the world's
// own sky boundary) with no other trees casting shade -- worth
// re-validating once real worldgen exists and light exposure varies by
// terrain rather than "how close to y=20 is the floor."
(
    name: "tree",
    // Mid green through yellow-green, on mid-to-red brown. Band 2 is the
    // original leaf palette and band 0 the original wood one, so an
    // individual drawing the low end of both ranges is exactly the tree
    // this file grew before bands existed -- the change adds neighbours,
    // it does not move the species.
    foliage_bands: (first: 2, count: 2),
    bark_bands: (first: 0, count: 2),
    // Stock fraction below which stomata start closing -- the drought-
    // caution policy genotype slot 7 multiplies per individual. 0.2 is a
    // first pass: at a fifth of a tank the plant starts trading growth
    // for endurance. 0.0 is the pre-closure engine exactly, and what any
    // species that does not declare this gets.
    stomatal_reserve: 0.2,
    cell_types: [
        (Seed, [
            Germinate(
                light_threshold: 0.1,
                // A tree is the largest commitment on this list -- it must fund a trunk before it earns anything -- so it waits for ground that will still be damp after the first dry spell.
                //
                // Plant-available soil water in the cell below, 0..1:
                // 0 is the permanent wilting point, 1 is field capacity.
                // Renamed from `moisture_threshold`, whose unit was the
                // field channel's 0..4 and which measured the wrong thing.
                soil_water_threshold: 0.25,
                instant: false,
            ),
        ]),
        (GrowingTip, [
            Grow(
                cost: 0.2,
                // **Per branch order** -- trunk, limb, branch, twig. See
                // organism::ByOrder: the list pads with its last value, so
                // a one-value list is a species with no tiers and a
                // two-value list is a shrub.
                //
                // A trunk that branches rarely under twigs that branch
                // often is what makes a bole with a crown on it, and it
                // costs no rule that has to decide which cells are trunk
                // -- a cell cannot know that locally, which is why four
                // successive support models failed trying to infer it from
                // shape.
                branch_chance: [0.03, 0.12, 0.2, 0.25],
                continuation_weight: 0.7,
                // Twigs chase light hardest; the trunk mostly ignores it
                // and goes up. Phototropism on the trunk is what made
                // early stems wander toward whichever side happened to be
                // brighter instead of standing up.
                light_weight: [0.15, 0.3, 0.5, 0.6],
                wind_weight: 0.2,
                // The trunk's straightness comes from here. Laterals get
                // progressively less of it, so a limb leaves the trunk and
                // stays out rather than curling back up alongside it --
                // which is what produced the parallel-ropes look.
                upward_weight: [0.9, 0.35, 0.1, 0.05],
                // Crown shyness -- keeps neighbouring crowns (and a crown's
                // own limbs) from merging. Owner-blind on purpose: see
                // `candidate_crowding` for the far-red reading.
                //
                // **The famous cliff is gone, and it was never ecology.**
                // This knob's history was "a narrow usable band one step
                // under a collapse" (median tree 2,620 cells at 12.0
                // against 26 at 20.0), which turned out to be arithmetic:
                // crowding was *subtracted* from the candidate score and
                // the score then filtered on `> 0`, so a strong weight did
                // not bias a crowded tip's choice, it emptied the choice --
                // and an emptied choice banks the stale ticks that retire a
                // lineage. Crowding *divides* the score now
                // (`plant.rs`, the candidate loop), so it reorders at any
                // strength and a crowded tip takes its least-bad direction.
                //
                // Swept on the divide form, 8 trees / 30,000 frames, cells /
                // thickest contiguous run above ground (57-cell spacing) --
                // shoot line only, roots pinned at 0.0:
                //
                //     6.0    36,307   76
                //    12.0    33,411   64
                //    20.0    36,303   52    <-- here
                //    30.0    34,976   61
                //
                // Stand mass is flat across the whole range -- the knob no
                // longer costs trees, it only moves separation -- and the
                // smallest tree at 20.0-equivalent is 2,811 cells where the
                // subtract form's was 26. Separation bottoms at
                // 20.0-equivalent and drifts back up at 30.0-equivalent;
                // nothing anywhere collapses.
                //
                // **30.0 here is the sweep's chosen 20.0, converted.**
                // `candidate_crowding` now reports density in fresh-deposit
                // units (divided by `GROW_CANOPY_DEPOSIT`, 1.5), so the
                // weight scales by 1.5 to hold the same behaviour; the
                // sweep table above is in the old raw unit. In deposit
                // units this weight survives changes to the deposit size
                // and the decay cadence, which are exactly what forced its
                // three historical re-derivations.
                crowding_weight: 30.0,
                max_active_tips: 14,
                // Every 3rd growth step along a shoot leaves a Leaf behind.
                // Untuned -- picked as the smallest interval that still
                // reads as "leaves spaced along a stem" rather than "every
                // cell is a leaf", and deliberately not tuned here: the one
                // economy pass this phase gets happens after polarity lands
                // (Reports/plant-substrate-v2-design.md §10 step 3), and
                // tuning it twice is exactly what that sequencing exists to
                // avoid.
                // **A bare bole falls out of this line.** The trunk leafs
                // every 12th step and a twig every other one, so foliage
                // ends up on the outside of the crown without any rule
                // about height or about what a "trunk" is. Not 0 on the
                // trunk: a seedling is order 0 and nothing else, so a
                // leafless trunk tier starves it before it can ever branch.
                plastochron: [12, 5, 2, 2],
                // A shoot only grows into open air. Reports/tree-rewrite-
                // design.md §5 scopes growing-into-material to roots.
                penetration_force: 0.0,
                // Turgor-limited height. See Behavior::Grow's own docs and
                // Reports/tree-extension-biology.md §2c: a shoot apex is
                // limited by water potential falling with height, not by
                // age or by running out of carbon.
                //
                // These three give a DERIVED ceiling rather than a cap:
                //   h_max = (source - yield) / per_cell = 0.9 / 0.0075 = 120 rows
                // Chosen so a mature tree sits well inside the harness sky
                // (200 rows) instead of pinning against the world's top
                // edge, which every scene has done so far and which voids
                // any shape number taken from it.
                // No two trees in a stand are the same tree. See
                // Behavior::Grow::genotype_variance -- 8 trees measuring
                // 127..131 rows tall was one tree drawn eight times.
                // Extension fades over the top 30% of the turgor range
                // instead of stopping on a line. See Behavior::Grow.
                turgor_taper: 0.3,
                // A seedling is order 0 and sees only the trunk tier, which
                // is the sparsest one by design. See Behavior::Grow's
                // juvenile_size: without this a seedling is handed the
                // bole's economy and starves before it can branch.
                // Momentum, so a stem sweeps instead of zig-zagging. See
                // OrganismCell::heading -- scoring continuation against a
                // one-cell local read is why growth looked erratic.
                heading_inertia: 0.85,
                // **Limbs leave the trunk wide and finer branches leave
                // shallower**, which is the real pattern and also the one
                // that opens a crown: a lateral departing at 70 degrees is
                // out of the trunk's own column immediately.
                //
                // Index 0 is read by a *trunk* cell making a limb, so this
                // list is "the angle at which each tier throws its
                // children", not "the angle this tier left at".
                branch_angle: [70.0, 60.0, 50.0, 45.0],
                // The trunk gets **no** straightness budget on purpose: it
                // should keep answering to light and gravity every step,
                // which is what stands it up. Everything else holds its
                // departure direction for a real run first -- that is the
                // difference between a limb and a meander, and without it
                // `branch_angle` is undone on the lateral's next step.
                internode: [0, 6, 4, 3],
                // A 2D cross-section shows every twig, so one leaf cell per
                // node renders as a bare skeleton with green specks. See
                // Behavior::Grow::leaf_cluster.
                // **Raised 5 -> 10, and it is a pure appearance knob now.**
                // Foliage measured 5% of a plant's cells across all three
                // species, so the silhouette was set by brown twig and no
                // architectural lever could reach it (Reports/plant-
                // appearance-design.md §2.1).
                //
                // Raising this used to be unaffordable: it quintupled income
                // and fused the stand. The node currency ended that -- income
                // (`intercepted / l_node`), the bud-break gate and the pipe
                // ratio (`q_peak / l_node`) are *all* normalised by
                // `leaf_cluster`, so a bigger cluster is more leaf cells at
                // the same economy. That normalisation was done to end the
                // constant treadmill; this is a dividend of it.
                //
                // Not free, and the two costs are real: every leaf cell
                // deposits canopy density, so crowding rises, and a denser
                // crown shades its own interior harder. Both are measured
                // paired against 5 rather than argued.
                leaf_cluster: 10,
                juvenile_size: 60,
                juvenile_plastochron: 0.25,
                juvenile_branch: 1.5,
                // [branch, upward, plastochron, turgor]. Widths set from a
                // 64-genome study: at a flat 0.15 only turgor correlated
                // with any outcome (r = -0.675 on height; the rest |r| <=
                // 0.13), and the plastochron's integer rounding erased its
                // variation entirely at the outer orders. The first three
                // are widened to clear that.
                // [branch, upward, plastochron, turgor, pipe_ratio, light].
                // Widths from a 1,024-genome study. Quintile spread in tree
                // size, low draw -> high draw:
                //   branch    823 -> 2078   (2.5x, live)
                //   plast    2443 ->  767   (3.2x, live, inverted)
                //   turgor   1709 -> 1162   (1.5x, and r = -0.75 on height)
                //   upward   1310 -> 1457   (flat -- INERT even at +/-40%)
                // Upward is held at 0.0 rather than removed: salts are
                // positional and renumbering rewrites every genome. The two
                // new slots are the traits with the most silhouette left in
                // them -- girth per unit foliage (stocky vs slender, until
                // now identical on every tree) and phototropic gain
                // (straight vs wandering).
                // Widths from two 1,024-genome studies. Quintile spread in
                // tree size, low draw -> high draw:
                //   plast    2459 ->  634   3.9x, the strongest trait
                //   branch    808 -> 2018   2.5x
                //   turgor   1477 -> 1176   1.3x on size, r = -0.74 height
                //   pipe     1537 -> 1247   weak, but stem 16.1 -> 12.7,
                //                           correctly signed -- so widened
                //   light    1386 1463 1369 1272 1394   DEAD at +/-50%
                //   upward   1310 1460 1396 1388 1457   DEAD at +/-40%
                //
                // Both dead traits are held at 0.0 rather than removed:
                // salts are positional and renumbering one rewrites every
                // genome ever measured.
                //
                // **Phototropism being inert is a finding, not a tuning
                // miss.** Since `field.rs` became a per-column sky cast the
                // light field is very nearly uniform above the canopy, so a
                // phototropic gradient has almost nothing to grab and
                // light_weight steers by noise. Worth fixing at the field,
                // not by widening this.
                //
                // branch is a genuine trade and 0.5 is the middle of it,
                // measured at both ends. At 0.6 the stand establishes well
                // and individuals drawing 1.6x fork near the ground, losing
                // the bole-then-crown silhouette. At 0.35 the survivors look
                // the best this branch has produced -- clean bole, rounded
                // crown -- and six of eight failed to establish at all,
                // against 35% failure stand-wide at 0.6.
                //
                // **The cause is worth writing down: a seedling branches to
                // build leaf area.** Branching is how a small plant gets
                // more shoots, more shoots is more foliage, and foliage is
                // the whole income -- so narrowing the trait removes exactly
                // the individuals that could establish. The real fix is not
                // a width: branch_chance should be high while juvenile and
                // low once established, which ByOrder cannot express,
                // because order is position in the plant and not age.
                //
                // **Re-mapped 2026-08-18** (Reports/plant-genome-design.md,
                // owner-signed): slots 1/5 now belong to the ROOT -- its
                // own vector on the RootTip entry carries them, they stay
                // 0.0 here and the shoot no longer reads them at all --
                // and three whole-plant slots extend the vector: 6
                // allocation bias, 7 stomatal closure (both read from
                // this vector by the passes that consume them), 8
                // penetration (root vector). Every study quoted above
                // remains true of slots 0/2/3/4, whose meanings did not
                // move; nothing else survives the re-map and the
                // megastudy re-baselines.
                genotype_variance: (0.5, 0.0, 0.4, 0.18, 0.7, 0.0, 0.4, 0.5, 0.0, 0.7),
                turgor_source: 1.0,
                turgor_yield: 0.1,
                turgor_per_cell: 0.0075,
            ),
            Photosynthesize(
                rate: 0.5,
                // A live tip photosynthesises but is not foliage to shed --
                // shedding pressure belongs to the Leaf entry below, and a
                // GrowingTip is the frontier itself.
                shade_death: 0.0,
                // Transpirational demand per tick at full light. A live tip
                // is foliage too and pays like one.
                transpiration: 0.05,
                // A tip is the frontier, not sheddable foliage -- the same
                // reasoning `shade_death: 0.0` above carries.
                drought_death: 0.0,
            ),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            Absorb(rate: 1.5),
            // **No `Transpire` here any more.** It drained adjacent soil
            // and credited nothing, which was the right stand-in while
            // water was not a currency -- it was the only thing making a
            // stand dry the ground at all.
            //
            // It is a double count now. Transpiration is a single path,
            // soil -> root -> leaf -> air: `Absorb` moves soil into the
            // plant and the foliage's `transpiration` demand moves it out
            // to the air. A second uncredited soil->air drain alongside
            // that corresponds to nothing, and it would make the ground dry
            // faster than the plants actually use it.
            //
            // If the resulting drawdown is too weak to steer roots
            // hydrotropically, the honest lever is the foliage demand, not
            // a phantom drain.

            Grow(
                cost: 0.25,
                // **Superseded by `branch_priming` below.** A root tip's
                // in-tick branch roll demanded a second step's carbon in
                // the tick the first was paid; it cleared that twice in
                // twelve thousand frames and fired zero times. Zero here
                // rather than left at its old value so nothing reads as a
                // live knob that no longer runs -- see
                // `Behavior::Grow::branch_priming`.
                branch_chance: [0.0],
                continuation_weight: 0.7,
                light_weight: [0.0],
                wind_weight: 0.0,
                upward_weight: [0.6],
                // 0.0 by design (no citation for root-root avoidance), and
                // **this is the file's second crowding line -- sweeps have
                // hit it twice.** First a blind sed on the field name
                // dragged this along with the shoot's value, so a whole
                // "shoot crowding" sweep silently varied root behaviour and
                // was thrown away. Then the occurrence-index script that
                // replaced the sed matched the *warning comment about the
                // first incident* (it contained the literal field-and-colon
                // text) while the sweep and a doc edit ran concurrently,
                // and mangled it. So, three rules with scars: a pattern
                // edit must prove it touched only its target; a comment
                // near a swept field must not contain the swept pattern;
                // and never edit a file a running sweep owns.
                crowding_weight: 0.0,
                max_active_tips: 10,
                // Roots grow by the same rule and must never leaf
                // underground -- 0 disables the mechanism entirely, which is
                // a real value here rather than "unset". See the field's own
                // doc in organism.rs.
                // Roots grow by the same rule and must never leaf
                // underground. One value, so every order agrees.
                plastochron: [0],
                // Steps between primed lateral sites, swept at landing over
                // 1/2/3/6/12/24: root mass falls monotonically as the
                // interval widens (755 cells at 1, 336 at 6) because a
                // sparser mark leaves the conversion with nothing available
                // to pick most ticks. 3 rather than 1 because 1 stops being
                // an oscillator -- it primes every cell, and the walk then
                // re-scans 82,487 primed cells over a run against 1,160 at
                // 3, for a stand-scale cost the mechanism does not need.
                // 3 also puts genotype slot 1 on the responsive part of the
                // curve, which is what lets the trait order an outcome at
                // all.
                branch_priming: [3],
                // **Roots read as small balls without this.** They run the
                // same Grow as a shoot, so they had the same one-cell
                // heading read and the same wandering -- but underground,
                // with no phototropism to straighten them and every
                // direction equally dark, the wander has nothing at all
                // pulling it into a line. Slightly less than the shoot's,
                // because a root really does follow moisture around
                // obstacles rather than driving straight.
                heading_inertia: 0.75,
                // Real root axial growth pressure is on the order of
                // 0.2-1.5 MPa; 1.2 puts this tree through loose soil (0.8)
                // and sand (1.4 -- refused), and never through gravel
                // (3.5) or any Solid. See Material::penetration_resistance.
                // With slot 8 below, that refusal is per *individual* now:
                // the strongest draws reach 1.5 and root in sand, and hard
                // ground bills extra carbon either way
                // (plant::penetration_cost_mult).
                penetration_force: 1.2,
                // **The root's own genome vector -- non-zero for the first
                // time.** Slots 1/5/8 are the root traits (the slot map on
                // organism::GENOTYPE_TRAITS is the contract): root branch
                // chance, root tropism gain (how hard it drives down and
                // toward water versus wandering), penetration. First-pass
                // widths for the paired sweeps: 1 matches the shoot's
                // branching width; 8's 0.25 keeps the weakest draw's force
                // at 0.9 -- above soil's 0.8, so no individual is locked
                // out of ordinary ground -- while the strongest reaches
                // 1.5, past sand's 1.4.
                genotype_variance: (0.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.4, 0.0, 0.0, 0.25, 0.0),
            ),
        ]),
        (MatureBody, [
            // **Set seed.** The rate is per mature cell, so a plant's
            // fecundity is its canopy size times this -- size buys
            // offspring with no rule saying so, which is what makes
            // selection on every other trait mean anything.
            //
            // Rates are a first pass and explicitly untuned against an
            // outcome: they want a sweep once a population has run long
            // enough to measure generations per unit time.
            Reproduce(seed_cost: 0.3, reproductive_allocation: 0.10, seed_maturity: 600),
            // **Nodes of foliage per cell of stem width** -- the pipe model
            // gate, in the `L_node` currency (`plant::INCOME_PER_NODE`'s
            // doc has the unit). 5.5 is the previous operating point
            // converted, not a re-tune: the gate compared `q_peak` in raw
            // summed light against 110, and `110 / L_node(=4.0 x 5)` is
            // 5.5 -- "a stem widens once it carries about five and a half
            // fully-lit nodes per cell of width", which is finally a
            // sentence a person can check against a picture.
            //
            // The number's history is the treadmill the unit ends, four
            // values through three meaning-changes: 10 (dividing by the
            // whole row's cells -- a limb elsewhere on the row suppressed
            // the trunk, 53% of rows had several runs), 45 (per-stem run
            // denominator, the first setting with a bare bole and a clear
            // fork; its sweep lived here for a long time), 22 (the
            // numerator became summed intercepted light when girth went
            // topological -- git 1874f3c has that sweep), 110 (leaf_cluster
            // quintupled the same light). The last two were pure unit
            // conversions; in nodes they never happen.
            //
            // Girth is bounded by crown size, which is the coupling the
            // pipe model exists for: trunks look thin because the crown is
            // sparse, and thicken on their own when it fills. Do not chase
            // trunk width by lowering this.
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 5.5),
            StructuralAnchor,
            // **Mature root tissue drinks.** This was `Transpire(rate:
            // 1.0)`, which pulled water out of the soil and credited it to
            // nothing -- correct while water was not a currency, and the
            // reason a stand could sit on a soil bed for 108,000 frames and
            // leave its median cell still at field capacity.
            //
            // `Absorb` here is what lets water income scale with root
            // *mass*. It was a `RootTip`-only behaviour, and root tips are
            // capped at 10 per plant, so income was bounded by a constant
            // while demand grows with a canopy of over a thousand leaves --
            // no constant balances that, because one side does not scale.
            //
            // Harmless on canopy MatureBody cells, which have no
            // water-bearing neighbour to draw from and so pay only the
            // four-neighbour look. Same rate as the tips: suberised tissue
            // really does take up less per cell, but there is far more of
            // it, and splitting the rate is a tuning question for the
            // economy sweep rather than a structural one.
            Absorb(rate: 1.5),
        ]),
        // The bud bank -- CellType::DormantBud's own doc has why extension
        // depositing buds, and nothing else depositing them, is the whole
        // mechanism. A bud is stem tissue, so it anchors and thickens like
        // any other MatureBody; the only thing it has that they do not is
        // one flush.
        (DormantBud, [
            BudBreak(
                // Roughly one growth step's worth. A flush should be a real
                // decision the plant pays for out of the same pool Grow
                // draws on, not free potential sitting on the shelf.
                cost: 0.2,
                // A one-in-five survival is what leaves a clear bole: a
                // trunk cell is thickened past many times over a tree's
                // life, so its buds are gone with near certainty, while a
                // twig that never thickens keeps all of them. Not 0 --
                // occasional survivors on a lower limb are what stop the
                // crown being a single flat layer at the top.
                thickening_survival: 0.2,
            ),
            StructuralAnchor,
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 5.5),
        ]),
        (Leaf, [
            // **Crown lift, finally on.** shade_death spent this branch's
            // whole history disabled, and the road to 0.03 is three
            // separate findings, each of which changed what the number
            // means:
            //
            // 1. As a threshold on raw light it was a nightly extinction
            //    event -- the light channel oscillates 20:1 over each day,
            //    every leaf reads near zero at midnight, and any fixed
            //    cutoff sheds the world. Fixed by the economy reading
            //    noon-equivalent light (phase-free by construction).
            // 2. The first sweeps of the phase-free gate read "any setting
            //    collapses the stand" -- a confound. Shedding used to
            //    schedule a structural check, and mid-crown checks amputate
            //    (the organism support search is hop-bounded, so high
            //    branches read as unsupported): 772 cells against 20,213 at
            //    the same rate, from that one line. Stranded sprays are now
            //    reclaimed by a leaves-only component walk instead
            //    (`plant::shed_stranded_leaves`).
            // 3. This is a **pressure, not a threshold**: the chance per
            //    organism tick that a leaf in total darkness is shed,
            //    falling with the cube of its light. A threshold on the
            //    phase-free reading genuinely works too (20,044 cells at
            //    cutoff 0.5); graded kept better crown separation (fused
            //    run 37 vs 55), a better-lit canopy, and thins a darkening
            //    region over ~a thousand frames instead of culling it on a
            //    line -- leaves going, not a shelf being swept.
            //
            // Swept with the full mechanism, 8 trees / 30,000 frames:
            // cells / leaves / median leaf light / thickest fused run
            // (57-cell spacing):
            //
            //     off     29,258   11,179   0.09   83
            //     0.01    21,964    3,614   1.64   51
            //     0.03    23,593    2,336   2.93   40    <-- here
            //     0.1     16,021    1,467   3.75   58
            //
            // 0.03 is a genuine interior optimum -- most mass AND best
            // separation AND a lit canopy -- which almost no sweep on this
            // branch has ever had. Off, two-thirds of all foliage sits
            // below 0.5 noon-equivalent light earning nothing, and that
            // dead interior mass is what re-fused the crowns. By eye
            // (target/filmstrips/p13-shed-grove.png): bare boles, foliage
            // tufted at the crown, the first sheet where wood reads as
            // wood.
            //
            // Then re-swept twice more, each time because the number's job
            // had changed under it. To 0.003 when four other mechanisms
            // took over crown separation (fusion is flat 0.03 -> 0.003 and
            // breaks only at 0.0; the commit that moved it has the table).
            // To 0.00075 in the WP-11 economy retune, because by then the
            // number's main output was not the crown but the FLOOR: with
            // litter and decay landed, leaf fall manufactures soil, and
            // the owner's verdict was "leaves are just falling too fast
            // ... creating a giant pile of soil". A cause counter
            // (`World::shed_shade`) showed this pressure is ~89% of all
            // leaf fall, so it is the lever. Swept 0.003 / 0.0015 /
            // 0.00075 on the colony scene at 12,000 frames: soil-writing
            // decay events 6,331 / 3,443 / 1,800, standing litter 1,081 /
            // 569 / 321, and crown separation held by eye at all three.
            // The owner picked the quarter rate on a three-arm card
            // (`20260823T161006584Z-6ecbab`: "C is best"). Two cautions
            // for the next retune: colony-band food *energy* does not drop
            // with the rate (retained foliage becomes low standing leaf --
            // medians 70k/82k/91k across the arms), and 0.00075 sits in
            // the previously unswept gap above 0.0 where fusion returns,
            // so a further cut needs the fused-run column re-measured.
            Photosynthesize(
                rate: 0.35,
                shade_death: 0.00075,
                // **Transpirational demand -- the charge that makes a root
                // worth having.** Before it, `Absorb` credited the same
                // pool `Photosynthesize` filled, so a rootless plant ran no
                // deficit and grew on light alone; that is what filled the
                // canopy with epiphytes.
                //
                // A first value, set gently on purpose so the mechanism can
                // be measured before it is tuned -- `CLAUDE.md`'s "run the
                // control that isolates it, at its gentlest setting". The
                // sweep that sets it properly is the economy re-derivation,
                // together with INCOME_PER_NODE and Grow.cost.
                transpiration: 0.05,
                // Drought shedding, the counterpart of shade_death and the
                // same order of magnitude. This is what makes a plant that
                // cannot reach soil thin out and die rather than stand
                // there inert -- the epiphyte's own death, from the economy
                // instead of from a germination guard.
                drought_death: 0.00075,
            ),
        ]),
    ],
)
